At sunset, the sky changes color due to the way light is scattered by the Earth's atmosphere. When sunlight passes through the Earth's atmosphere, it encounters many molecules of gases and dust in the air. These particles scatter or "scatter" some of the shorter blue and green wavelengths of light more than the longer red and orange wavelengths.

This scattering occurs because smaller particles have a larger surface area relative to their volume compared to larger particles. As light travels through the atmosphere, it experiences multiple interactions with these tiny particles as it moves from the sun toward the observer on the ground. Some of this scattering can happen even when objects like trees or buildings are not in direct line of sight.

When you look up at the sky at sunset, what you're seeing isn't actually coming straight down from the sun;

